  "answer": "To prevent an agent from experiencing a high improvement penalty and lowered actual performance due to inaccurate Q-value estimation, it has been proposed to apply reroute constrained policy improvement (RBI) during off-policy batch reinforcement learning fine-tuning on static datasets (https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.07805). The mechanism works by controlling the action probability shifts between the new policy and the behavior policy (https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.07805). Because mathematical derivation shows the variance of the improvement penalty is proportional to |β−π|²/β, RBI applies a multiplicative reroute constraint that restricts the policy to π(a|s)=c(s,a)β(a|s) within the limits of cmin≤c≤cmax, directly bounding this critical ratio (https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.07805). Utilizing the Max-Reroute algorithm, this is solved as a statewise linear program for the optimal policy under the reroute constraint, which guarantees monotonic advantage improvement and guards against the effects of uncertain estimates (https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.07805)."
